<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kshatriya">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kshatriya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kshatriya is the second of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> of the traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> social order, the class of rulers and warriors, ranked after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. Vedic and later texts assign to Kshatriyas the duties of protecting the people, governing and waging war. Many ruling families in Indian history, including dynasties of other origins, claimed Kshatriya status to legitimise their rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clans traced their descent from the solar and lunar lineages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kshatriya.jpg" alt="Kshatriya" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Friederisk · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Bhishma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> kshatra, meaning rule or authority, and the class is also called Rajanya. In early Rigvedic society the administration was headed by a tribal king, the Rajan, whose position may or may not have been hereditary. He may have been elected in a tribal assembly, the Samiti, protected the tribe and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, was assisted by a priest and did not keep a standing army. The fourfold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> system is not yet recorded in this period.</p><p>The Purusha Sukta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> describes the symbolic creation of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> through cosmic sacrifice. It uses the term rajanya rather than Kshatriya and is the first extant Vedic text to name the four classes together; some scholars consider it a late addition to the Rigveda. By the end of the Vedic period the word rajanya, which stressed kinship with the ruler, had been replaced by Kshatriya, which denotes power over a specific domain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, from 800 to 700 BCE, the rank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> was uncertain. The Panchavimsha Brahmana places the Rajanya first, before the Brahmana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">Vaishya</a>, while one passage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a> puts them second and another puts them third. The order Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a> became fixed from the time of the dharmasutras, between 450 and 100 BCE.</p><p>Kshatriyas were often considered pre-eminent in Buddhist circles. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> society they were sometimes rivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, but they generally acknowledged the superiority of the priestly class. Their ritual duties were to study the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, give gifts and perform fire sacrifice, and in ritual the staff of the banyan tree is assigned to them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>During the period of the Mahajanapadas, about 600 to 300 BCE, the oligarchic republics called gana sanghas were ruled by Kshatriya clans. Brahmanical sources sometimes called these clans degenerate Kshatriyas or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>, since they did not follow the Vedic religion, and they were often patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>. After that period most prominent royal dynasties in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were not Kshatriyas; the rulers of the Nanda Empire were said to be Shudras.</p><p>Between 300 and 700 CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> bestowed Kshatriya status on new royal dynasties by linking them to the Kshatriyas of the epics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, through the Solar dynasty (Suryavamsha) and the Lunar dynasty (Chandravamsha). In return the new Kshatriyas patronised and rewarded the Brahmins. Other claimed lineages include the Agnivanshi, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Agni</a>, and the Nagavanshi, descended from the Nagas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kshatriya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruler bore Hindu titles, raja-di-raja, king of kings, or pu po tana raya, lord of all territories, and regnal names formed from prefixes such as Jaya, Maha and Sri with the -varman suffix of the <b>Kshatriya</b> class.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Chenla's kings took the Sanskrit name of a patron deity or avatar with the suffix -varman, &quot;protected by&quot;, following the code of the <b>Kshatriya</b> caste in the Laws of Manu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…January 1750 Marthanda Varma dedicated the state to Padmanabha, and the kings ruled as his servants; they were conditionally promoted to <b>Kshatriya</b> status through sixteen mahādānams, until in 1848 Lord Dalhousie warned Uttram Tirunal that Madras would take over the administration if the costly practice did not end.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The law books describe four varnas, or classes: Brahmins, priests and teachers; <b>Kshatriyas</b>, rulers and warriors; Vaishyas, merchants and farmers; and Shudras, labourers and servants.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Early on the tribal chief (rajan) and nobility (rajanya) stood at the top, the common people (viś) in the middle and the dasa and dasyu at the bottom; the Rigveda does not yet link the words Brahmana and <b>Kshatriya</b> to varna, and Vaishya and Shudra are absent.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the battle Arjuna, seeing his kin and teachers in the opposing army, refuses to fight, and his charioteer Krishna reminds him of his duty as a <b>Kshatriya</b>; this dialogue is the Bhagavad Gita.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
